CCC-ARCHGATE: ARCHITECTURE-NEUTRAL GATEKEEPER
​1. PURPOSE & CORE DEFINITION
​ccc-archgate is a standalone, architecture-neutral executable component
included with the ccc C/C++ compiler toolchain.
​Its sole purpose is to act as an active runtime gatekeeper during compilation
and build execution: determining the target architecture in context and
switching architecture-specific settings cleanly while work is in progress.
​2. ZERO CONFIG FILES, ZERO REGISTRIES
​Unlike traditional cross-toolchain wrappers that rely on external config
files, environment soup, or target registries, ccc-archgate requires none.
​There are no registry lookups, no external path parsers, and no fragile
host-dependent configuration files to drift out of sync.
​All target rules, ABI invariants, and architecture switches are self-contained
and resolved deterministically at runtime from direct invocation context and
binary metadata.
​3. WHY IT IS ARCHITECTURE-NEUTRAL
​ccc-archgate separates decision logic from architecture payloads:
​The gatekeeper engine itself is written in strict, portable C89 with zero
hardcoded assumptions about host word size, pointer width, or CPU family.
​Because the gate logic is completely agnostic to the underlying CPU, the
component itself compiles and runs natively on any host architecture
(x86_64, aarch64, riscv64, etc.).
​Architecture-specific parameters (endianness, ABI alignment, sysroot paths,
and target triple flags) are treated purely as swappable state profiles
activated only when the gate switches target context.
​4. DETERMINISTIC BINARY DATA MODEL
​Whenever ccc-archgate inspects or exchanges target metadata (such as
interfacing with runepkg target structures or target ELF artifacts), it
relies on explicit, fixed-width binary serialization (uint16_t, uint32_t,
uint64_t with magic headers) rather than raw C struct dumps.
​This guarantees identical behavior across compilation units and eliminates
struct alignment, padding, and ABI discrepancies across architectures.
​5. ROLE IN THE COMPILATION PIPELINE
​1. Intercepts build/compiler work before architecture-sensitive stages execute.
​2. Evaluates the requested or active target context without external configs.
​3. Validates target compatibility (preventing host/target ABI bleed).
​4. Switches active architecture-specific settings in-flight for downstream
compiler, assembler, linker, or runas execution stages.
